1
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:04,473
Now last but not least, to sum up this

2
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:07,472
basics refresher, let's talk about generics.

3
00:00:08,049 --> 00:00:10,663
Generics are a feature that can look very

4
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:12,876
confusing at first, but it makes a lot of

5
00:00:12,876 --> 00:00:13,229
sense.

6
00:00:14,127 --> 00:00:16,564
Actually, in our code here, we already have

7
00:00:16,564 --> 00:00:17,446
a generic type—

8
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:19,306
our 2 array types here.

9
00:00:20,156 --> 00:00:22,449
A generic type simply is a type that

10
00:00:22,545 --> 00:00:25,977
interacts with another type. And an array is

11
00:00:26,009 --> 00:00:26,907
a great example.

12
00:00:27,757 --> 00:00:30,418
An array is a type on its own. It's a list

13
00:00:30,418 --> 00:00:32,277
of data. That's the core type.

14
00:00:33,047 --> 00:00:35,884
But it interacts with another type, the type

15
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:38,481
of data inside of the array. So you could

16
00:00:38,513 --> 00:00:41,286
say the array is the outer type, but then

17
00:00:41,319 --> 00:00:43,771
you have all the elements in the array as an

18
00:00:44,028 --> 00:00:44,621
inner type.

19
00:00:45,871 --> 00:00:48,372
And this here, this way of defining an array

20
00:00:48,372 --> 00:00:50,584
type, is actually just a shortcut in

21
00:00:50,616 --> 00:00:53,583
TypeScript. The longer form would be that we

22
00:00:53,583 --> 00:00:55,944
set numResults to type array.

23
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:59,220
But now array is a so-called generic type

24
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:00,843
and you have to define

25
00:01:01,437 --> 00:01:04,585
the wrapped, the inner type, in this case,

26
00:01:04,906 --> 00:01:08,119
the value types of the values inside of the

27
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:08,360
array.

28
00:01:08,922 --> 00:01:11,251
And you do so by adding angle brackets here,

29
00:01:11,556 --> 00:01:13,708
the smaller than and the greater than sign,

30
00:01:14,142 --> 00:01:17,194
and between that, the generic type of array.

31
00:01:17,387 --> 00:01:20,467
So in this case, number. And this says that

32
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:22,695
numResults is an array full of numbers.

33
00:01:23,608 --> 00:01:26,284
Now, what the thing between angle brackets

34
00:01:26,332 --> 00:01:26,989
refers to

35
00:01:27,534 --> 00:01:29,825
depends on the generic type you're working

36
00:01:29,857 --> 00:01:30,002
with.

37
00:01:30,578 --> 00:01:33,046
Another example for a generic type would be

38
00:01:33,062 --> 00:01:33,719
a promise.

39
00:01:34,344 --> 00:01:36,715
Now, for that, we can create a new promise

40
00:01:36,748 --> 00:01:38,847
down there, myPromise, by calling new

41
00:01:38,991 --> 00:01:39,472
Promise.

42
00:01:40,417 --> 00:01:42,356
And by default, this code won't work here

43
00:01:42,596 --> 00:01:45,080
because we need to add a library to

44
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,069
tsconfig. To basically tell tsconfig

45
00:01:48,841 --> 00:01:50,757
which kind of features we wanna support.

46
00:01:51,658 --> 00:01:53,735
And for example, by default here, because of

47
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:54,475
this target,

48
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:56,989
it's configured to compile the TypeScript

49
00:01:56,989 --> 00:01:59,877
code down to ES5 JavaScript code, which is

50
00:01:59,974 --> 00:02:00,526
quite old.

51
00:02:01,126 --> 00:02:05,577
If we pick ES6 here, for example,

52
00:02:07,746 --> 00:02:09,963
now this will work. We still get an error,

53
00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:11,166
but now it's a different error.

54
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,865
So we just have to ensure that we're telling

55
00:02:13,898 --> 00:02:15,646
TypeScript that we're working with a

56
00:02:15,646 --> 00:02:17,973
JavaScript version that supports Promise

57
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:20,074
because Promise of course is not a

58
00:02:20,074 --> 00:02:22,641
TypeScript feature. It can't be compiled to

59
00:02:22,641 --> 00:02:24,743
something that works in older JavaScript.

60
00:02:25,032 --> 00:02:27,134
Instead, we need to tell TypeScript that we

61
00:02:27,214 --> 00:02:29,588
wanna compile to JavaScript which natively

62
00:02:29,621 --> 00:02:31,594
supports Promise and that's what we just did

63
00:02:31,851 --> 00:02:33,920
by changing the target to ES6.

64
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:37,033
With that, we can define our Promise here.

65
00:02:37,791 --> 00:02:38,890
And a promise gets

66
00:02:39,893 --> 00:02:41,979
an argument which is a function,

67
00:02:43,693 --> 00:02:45,989
and that function itself gets 2 arguments, a

68
00:02:46,118 --> 00:02:47,922
resolve and a reject function.

69
00:02:48,549 --> 00:02:50,622
And inside of the promise, when we define it

70
00:02:50,702 --> 00:02:51,248
on our own,

71
00:02:51,843 --> 00:02:53,658
we can for example set a timeout

72
00:02:54,494 --> 00:02:57,293
to execute some code after a second, and

73
00:02:57,455 --> 00:02:59,599
after that second we resolve the promise

74
00:02:59,712 --> 00:03:01,293
with it worked.

75
00:03:02,954 --> 00:03:04,921
And then we can use our own promise here

76
00:03:06,698 --> 00:03:08,572
to listen to the result

77
00:03:09,983 --> 00:03:12,819
and console.log it here. And we could also

78
00:03:12,947 --> 00:03:15,447
add catch to react to the rejection of the

79
00:03:15,447 --> 00:03:15,736
promise.

80
00:03:16,393 --> 00:03:18,925
And now let's compile this. By the way, now

81
00:03:19,053 --> 00:03:21,328
with a config file, don't select the file

82
00:03:21,761 --> 00:03:22,226
manually,

83
00:03:22,899 --> 00:03:24,774
otherwise the config file won't be taken

84
00:03:24,822 --> 00:03:26,921
into account by the compiler. It is taken

85
00:03:26,921 --> 00:03:28,652
into account by the IDE but not by the

86
00:03:28,652 --> 00:03:29,117
compiler.

87
00:03:29,806 --> 00:03:33,140
Instead, just press tsc like this, enter tsc

88
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:34,552
like this, and it will compile all

89
00:03:34,744 --> 00:03:36,043
TypeScript files in the folder.

90
00:03:36,845 --> 00:03:39,363
And now it compiled this code. And if we now

91
00:03:39,635 --> 00:03:40,181
reload,

92
00:03:41,255 --> 00:03:43,067
you see it worked after 1 second.

93
00:03:43,901 --> 00:03:45,986
But now why is the promise a generic type?

94
00:03:46,788 --> 00:03:49,257
Because it eventually resolves to a value,

95
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:51,262
and the value it resolves to,

96
00:03:51,823 --> 00:03:54,405
that's the generic type for the promise. For

97
00:03:54,421 --> 00:03:56,650
the array, it was the value stored in the

98
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:58,734
array. For the promise, it's the value the

99
00:03:59,055 --> 00:04:01,251
promise resolves to. And for example, here

100
00:04:01,299 --> 00:04:02,533
it resolves to a string

101
00:04:03,062 --> 00:04:04,777
and hence we should be able to call

102
00:04:05,867 --> 00:04:07,357
split here, for example,

103
00:04:08,111 --> 00:04:09,281
and split on

104
00:04:10,739 --> 00:04:11,188
the W.

105
00:04:11,877 --> 00:04:13,801
But this does not work because TypeScript

106
00:04:13,801 --> 00:04:16,093
does not understand that this is a string.

107
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:18,208
It does not know the type.

108
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:21,174
Well, by adding angle brackets after

109
00:04:21,174 --> 00:04:24,251
promise, we can set the type to which

110
00:04:24,283 --> 00:04:26,479
promise will resolve to to a string.

111
00:04:27,264 --> 00:04:29,717
Now you can't set angle brackets on every

112
00:04:29,829 --> 00:04:32,025
built-in object. It needs to be an object

113
00:04:32,154 --> 00:04:34,718
that supports this. But the Promise object,

114
00:04:34,799 --> 00:04:36,931
the Promise constructor function, does

115
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:39,832
support generic types because you can set

116
00:04:39,832 --> 00:04:41,900
the value the Promise will eventually

117
00:04:41,932 --> 00:04:42,509
resolve to.

118
00:04:43,054 --> 00:04:45,475
And now you see that split down there works.

119
00:04:46,164 --> 00:04:46,613
And if I

120
00:04:47,206 --> 00:04:48,552
recompile this and reload,

121
00:04:49,130 --> 00:04:52,079
we see an array. It irked because of split.

122
00:04:52,657 --> 00:04:55,840
So generic types can be tricky the first

123
00:04:55,888 --> 00:04:58,379
time you see them, but they make a lot of

124
00:04:58,379 --> 00:05:01,642
sense and they give you extra type safety

125
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:04,809
when working with more complex types or

126
00:05:04,809 --> 00:05:07,059
types that are simply connected to each

127
00:05:07,059 --> 00:05:07,220
other.
